course: dictionary
lesson: 5018
title: Who is an influencer?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An influencer is a person who changes what other people think or do, often through social media. Some influencers are paid by companies to show their products, and others are simply followed by a lot of people.
explain: Read the words around the word for clues. «Influencers are often paid to praise products; look for #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership».» The words paid and praise tell you that the praise may be bought, so the post is an advert.
explain: Ask yourself: who gains if I buy this? A boy in England bought a gadget after a video, but it was a paid advert, and the gadget broke in a week. Being paid to praise something is not the same as a fair test of how good it is.
fact: An influencer is a person who is paid by companies or popular on social media to make people want to buy or do things.
fact: An influencer can be any person who changes what others think or do.
fact: Influencers are often paid to praise products, so their praise can be an advert.
fact: Look for #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership» to spot a paid post.
fact: Reading reviews from ordinary buyers helps you check a product fairly.
exercise: «Influencers are often paid to praise products; look for #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership».» What does #ad show? | That the video is very old | That the post is a joke | That the post is about a famous footballer | *That the post is a paid advert
exercise: What is an influencer? | A kind of petrol station | *A person who is paid by companies or popular online to make people want to buy or do things | A tool for cutting paper | A person who never uses the internet
exercise: Why might an influencer's praise for a gadget not be a fair test? | Because influencers only talk about food | *Because they may have been paid to praise it | Because gadgets never work | Because praise is always false
test: Why should you read reviews from ordinary buyers? | *To check a product fairly, beyond paid praise | To see which influencer is the oldest | To learn the name of the company's boss | To find out the price of the product only
test: «A boy in England paid for an influencer's favourite gadget after a video; it was a paid advert, and the gadget broke in a week.» What is the lesson? | Videos are always honest | Gadgets always last for years | *Paid praise can hide a poor product | Influencers are never paid
exam: Before you trust an influencer's praise, what should you check for? | The number of likes on your own phone | The weather in the video | Their age and their hair colour | *Signs such as #ad or «sponsored» that show the post is paid
